1
00:00:00,267 --> 00:00:01,633
ADHD isn't a lack

2
00:00:01,633 --> 00:00:03,633
of attention. It's trouble

3
00:00:03,633 --> 00:00:04,567
steering it.

4
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:06,867
Meet Noor. Give her

5
00:00:06,867 --> 00:00:08,367
a hobby she loves,

6
00:00:08,367 --> 00:00:09,933
and hours disappear.

7
00:00:10,667 --> 00:00:12,000
But one boring email?

8
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:13,267
She can't even start

9
00:00:13,267 --> 00:00:13,533
it.

10
00:00:14,267 --> 00:00:16,233
About six percent of

11
00:00:16,233 --> 00:00:18,200
US adults have ADHD,

12
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:19,933
the CDC estimates.

13
00:00:20,667 --> 00:00:22,267
Many researchers see it

14
00:00:22,267 --> 00:00:23,433
as a problem of

15
00:00:23,433 --> 00:00:25,633
regulating attention, not missing

16
00:00:25,633 --> 00:00:25,933
it.

17
00:00:26,667 --> 00:00:28,267
People with ADHD report

18
00:00:28,267 --> 00:00:30,233
hyperfocus more often, though

19
00:00:30,233 --> 00:00:31,600
that's self-reported.

20
00:00:32,333 --> 00:00:33,833
It runs in families:

21
00:00:33,833 --> 00:00:35,933
twin studies put heritability

22
00:00:35,933 --> 00:00:37,767
near seventy-four percent.

23
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:39,667
So just focus is

24
00:00:39,667 --> 00:00:41,200
never the whole story.

25
00:00:41,933 --> 00:00:44,500
Only a qualified professional

26
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:46,367
can diagnose ADHD. A

27
00:00:46,367 --> 00:00:47,533
video can't.

28
00:00:48,233 --> 00:00:50,033
If this sounds familiar,

29
00:00:50,033 --> 00:00:51,600
talk to a clinician.

30
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:52,867
Follow for more.
